{ignore}
<p><code>{count 'orders'}</code> is an "is there anything in it" test: its content renders when
the data set or the page array named <code>orders</code> holds at least one element, and its
<code>@else@</code> part renders when it is empty. A typical use is a heading and a table that
only appear when there are rows, with a "nothing found" text otherwise.</p>

<p>Despite its name the tag prints no number. The number of rows of a loop is the
<code>count@orders</code> property, readable inside the loop and in conditions. The name is
looked up as a <a href="?tag/data">{data}</a> set first, then as a variable of the page. A
variable that does not exist, or is NULL, holds nothing; one that holds a single plain value
counts as holding that value.</p>

<p>Write the name quoted: <code>{count 'orders'}</code>. <code>{count $orders}</code> evaluates
the array itself as the name, which is an error. Inside a loop, the data tag's own
<code>@else@</code> part already shows when it has no rows - <code>{count}</code> is for
markup <em>around</em> the loop.</p>
{/ignore}
